const FINAL_ANSWERS = {"answers":{"parkinsons-disease-psychiatric-aspects":{"title":"Psychiatric manifestations of Parkinson's disease and their management","status":"draft","updated":"2026-10-15","reviewer":[],"covers":["p3-303f02bd","p3-fdf72d0c","p3-f4a09b39","p3-a0ea515e","p3-dc17344a","p3-868b6f08"],"exam":"<p><strong>Definition.</strong> Parkinson&#39;s disease (PD) is a progressive α-synucleinopathy in which neuropsychiatric syndromes — depression, anxiety, apathy, psychosis, impulse-control disorders, cognitive impairment and sleep disorders — are common, often precede the motor signs, and arise from three sources: the disease itself, its treatment (dopaminergic drugs, deep-brain stimulation), and the psychological response to a chronic disabling illness.</p>\n<h3>Mechanism</h3>\n<p><strong>Braak staging:</strong> α-synuclein pathology spreads in a predictable sequence.</p>\n<p>Stage 1–2: dorsal vagal nucleus, olfactory bulb, <strong>locus coeruleus (noradrenaline), raphe (serotonin)</strong> → hyposmia, constipation, RBD, depression, anxiety (prodromal)\n→ Stage 3–4: <strong>substantia nigra</strong>, basal forebrain, amygdala → motor syndrome, early emotional and cognitive change\n→ Stage 5–6: limbic and neocortical spread → dementia, psychosis.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Dopamine loss</strong> (nigrostriatal, mesolimbic, mesocortical) → apathy, anhedonia, executive dysfunction.</li>\n<li><strong>Dopaminergic drugs</strong> over-stimulating the relatively preserved ventral striatum → impulse-control disorders, psychosis.</li>\n<li><strong>Noradrenaline and serotonin loss</strong> → depression, anxiety.</li>\n<li><strong>Cholinergic loss</strong> (nucleus basalis of Meynert) → dementia, visual hallucinations.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Clinical syndromes</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Syndrome</th>\n<th>Frequency</th>\n<th>Key features</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Depression</td>\n<td>MDD ~17%; clinically significant depressive symptoms ~35%</td>\n<td>Anhedonia, pessimism, less guilt than primary depression; may precede motor signs; mood dips in OFF periods</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Anxiety</td>\n<td>~31%</td>\n<td>GAD, panic, social anxiety, fear of falling; <strong>OFF-period panic</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Apathy</td>\n<td>~40%</td>\n<td>Loss of motivation <strong>without sadness</strong>; worse after agonist withdrawal or STN-DBS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Psychosis</td>\n<td>Hallucinations in up to 50% over the illness</td>\n<td>Minor phenomena (presence, passage, illusions) → well-formed visual hallucinations with insight → delusions (persecutory, jealousy) without insight</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Impulse-control disorders</td>\n<td>~14%</td>\n<td>Gambling, hypersexuality, compulsive buying, binge eating; <strong>punding</strong>; <strong>dopamine dysregulation syndrome</strong>; linked to dopamine agonists</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cognitive impairment</td>\n<td>PD-MCI ~25%; dementia in ~80% after 20 years</td>\n<td>Executive, attention, visuospatial; retrieval more than storage memory deficit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sleep</td>\n<td>RBD ~40–50%</td>\n<td>Dream enactment; insomnia; daytime sleepiness and sleep attacks with agonists</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p><strong>Iatrogenic syndromes</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Dopamine agonist withdrawal syndrome (DAWS):</strong> anxiety, panic, dysphoria, pain and craving on tapering an agonist; does not respond to levodopa.</li>\n<li><strong>After STN-DBS:</strong> transient hypomania or impulsivity early; later apathy and depression as drugs are reduced; increased suicide risk.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Assessment</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>History from <strong>patient and caregiver</strong> (patients hide ICDs); relate each symptom to motor onset, drug changes and <strong>ON/OFF timing</strong>; ask about suicide.</li>\n<li>Scales: MDS-UPDRS Part I; GDS-15 or HADS (depression); Parkinson Anxiety Scale; Starkstein Apathy Scale; <strong>QUIP</strong> (impulse-control disorders); <strong>MoCA</strong> (cognition); SCOPA-PC (psychosis).</li>\n<li>Investigations to exclude delirium and mimics: CBC, sodium and electrolytes, renal and liver function, TSH, B12, glucose, urine; imaging, EEG or polysomnography when indicated.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Management</h3>\n<p><strong>General principles:</strong> optimise motor fluctuations first → rationalise drugs before adding new ones → choose psychotropics that do not worsen parkinsonism, falls or cognition → involve the neurologist and the family.</p>\n<p><strong>Depression</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>SSRIs (sertraline, escitalopram, paroxetine) or SNRI (venlafaxine) — effective without motor worsening in the <strong>SAD-PD</strong> trial.</li>\n<li>Nortriptyline effective but anticholinergic; pramipexole has an antidepressant effect but raises ICD risk.</li>\n<li><strong>CBT</strong> effective; <strong>ECT</strong> for severe, psychotic or suicidal depression (often transiently improves motor symptoms).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Anxiety:</strong> smooth dopaminergic delivery for OFF-period anxiety; SSRIs/SNRIs; CBT; avoid long-term benzodiazepines.</p>\n<p><strong>Apathy:</strong> exclude depression and dementia; optimise dopamine; rivastigmine; behavioural activation.</p>\n<p><strong>Psychosis — stepwise</strong></p>\n<p>Treat <strong>delirium</strong> precipitants (infection, dehydration, hyponatraemia, new drugs)\n→ withdraw drugs in this order: anticholinergics → amantadine → MAO-B inhibitors → dopamine agonists → COMT inhibitors → reduce levodopa last\n→ if still distressing: <strong>clozapine 6.25–50 mg/day</strong> (best evidence; blood monitoring) or <strong>pimavanserin 34 mg/day</strong> (5-HT2A inverse agonist); quetiapine widely used but weak evidence\n→ add <strong>rivastigmine</strong> if cognitive impairment.</p>\n<p><strong>Never</strong> use haloperidol, risperidone or olanzapine — they worsen parkinsonism and can cause severe sensitivity reactions.</p>\n<p><strong>Impulse-control disorders:</strong> <strong>reduce or stop the dopamine agonist</strong> (most effective step), taper slowly and watch for DAWS; compensate with levodopa; CBT, financial safeguards, spouse involvement; supervise levodopa supply in DDS.</p>\n<p><strong>Cognitive impairment:</strong> <strong>rivastigmine</strong> (EXPRESS trial) or donepezil; stop anticholinergics; treat vascular risk and sleep; capacity and driving assessment.</p>\n<p><strong>RBD:</strong> bedroom safety; melatonin first; clonazepam with caution.</p>\n<p><strong>DBS:</strong> psychiatric screening before surgery; monitor mood, apathy, impulsivity and suicide afterwards.</p>\n<h3>Key evidence</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>SAD-PD (2012):</strong> paroxetine and venlafaxine XR improved depression in PD.</li>\n<li><strong>Parkinson Study Group (1999):</strong> low-dose clozapine improved psychosis without motor worsening.</li>\n<li><strong>Pimavanserin phase 3 (2014):</strong> improved PD psychosis; FDA approved 2016.</li>\n<li><strong>EXPRESS (2004):</strong> rivastigmine modestly improved cognition in PD dementia.</li>\n<li><strong>DOMINION (2010):</strong> ICDs in about 14%; dopamine agonists raise the odds 2–3.5-fold.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Pitfalls</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Hypomimia, bradyphrenia and fatigue mimic depression; apathy is not depression.</li>\n<li>New confusion with hallucinations is <strong>delirium until proved otherwise</strong>.</li>\n<li>Dementia within a year of parkinsonism suggests <strong>dementia with Lewy bodies</strong>, not PD dementia.</li>\n<li>Ask about gambling and sexual behaviour every visit when a patient takes an agonist.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Conclusion.</strong> Psychiatric syndromes are intrinsic to Parkinson&#39;s disease and its treatment, not incidental complications. Good management links each symptom to disease stage, motor fluctuations and medication, rationalises drugs before adding new ones, and uses motor-sparing treatments — SSRIs/SNRIs and CBT for depression, clozapine or pimavanserin for psychosis, agonist reduction for impulse-control disorders, and cholinesterase inhibitors for dementia.</p>\n","depth":"<h3>Background</h3>\n<p>James Parkinson&#39;s 1817 <em>Essay on the Shaking Palsy</em> described the senses and intellect as uninjured — a view now known to be wrong. Recognition of PD as a multisystem disorder with a long non-motor prodrome grew from Braak&#39;s neuropathological staging (2003) and from cohort studies showing that depression, anxiety, RBD, hyposmia and constipation often precede motor diagnosis by years. Non-motor symptoms are now recognised as the main determinants of quality of life, caregiver strain and institutional care, yet they are missed in a large proportion of routine neurology consultations.</p>\n<h3>Epidemiology</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>PD affects roughly 1% of people over 60 and over 6 million people worldwide (Global Burden of Disease 2016); numbers are rising with population ageing.</li>\n<li>In India, community prevalence estimates vary widely; the highest reported was among the Parsi community of Mumbai (Bharucha et al., 1988). Most Indian estimates are lower than Western figures, partly because of methodology and under-ascertainment.</li>\n<li>Sources of the frequencies in the exam answer: depression — Reijnders et al., 2008 (systematic review); anxiety — Broen et al., 2016 (meta-analysis); apathy — den Brok et al., 2015 (meta-analysis); ICDs — DOMINION, 2010; PD-MCI — Aarsland et al., 2010 (pooled analysis); dementia — Sydney multicentre study, Hely et al., 2008; psychosis — Forsaa et al., 2010 (cross-sectional prevalence 20–40%, cumulative up to ~60% over long follow-up).</li>\n<li>Risk factors for ICDs: younger age at onset, male sex (gambling, hypersexuality), personal or family history of addiction, novelty-seeking traits, depression, higher agonist dose.</li>\n<li>Risk factors for psychosis: older age, longer illness, cognitive impairment, visual impairment, RBD, higher dopaminergic load.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Classification and criteria</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>ICD-11</strong> codes these as <em>secondary</em> mental or behavioural syndromes (e.g. secondary mood syndrome, secondary psychotic syndrome) alongside the PD code. <strong>DSM-5-TR</strong> uses &quot;depressive disorder due to another medical condition&quot;, &quot;psychotic disorder due to another medical condition&quot;, and &quot;major or mild neurocognitive disorder due to Parkinson&#39;s disease&quot;.</li>\n<li><strong>PD psychosis criteria</strong> (NINDS/NIMH workgroup, Ravina et al., 2007): illusions, false sense of presence, hallucinations or delusions; onset after PD diagnosis; recurrent or continuous for at least one month; not better explained by delirium, schizophrenia, DLB or another cause.</li>\n<li><strong>One-year rule:</strong> dementia beginning more than a year after established parkinsonism = PD dementia; before or within a year = dementia with Lewy bodies. It is a clinical convention — both are Lewy body diseases.</li>\n<li><strong>MDS criteria</strong> exist for PD-MCI (Litvan et al., 2012) and PD dementia (Emre et al., 2007).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Mechanism in detail</h3>\n<p><strong>Neurotransmitter systems</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>System</th>\n<th>Lesion</th>\n<th>Psychiatric consequence</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Dopamine (disease)</td>\n<td>Nigrostriatal, mesolimbic, mesocortical loss</td>\n<td>Apathy, anhedonia, executive dysfunction, OFF-period dysphoria</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dopamine (drugs)</td>\n<td>Preserved ventral striatum over-stimulated</td>\n<td>ICDs, punding, psychosis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Noradrenaline</td>\n<td>Locus coeruleus</td>\n<td>Anxiety, depression, attention deficits</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Serotonin</td>\n<td>Raphe nuclei</td>\n<td>Depression, anxiety</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Acetylcholine</td>\n<td>Nucleus basalis of Meynert, pedunculopontine nucleus</td>\n<td>Dementia, visual hallucinations, falls</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p><strong>Visual hallucinations — Perception and Attention Deficit model (Collerton et al., 2005).</strong> Impaired visual processing (retinal dopamine loss, ventral visual stream dysfunction) combined with impaired attentional control (cholinergic deficit) allows an incorrect &quot;proto-object&quot; to be perceived as real; intrusion of REM-type imagery into wakefulness contributes. Dopaminergic drugs lower the threshold but are not the sole cause, which explains why hallucinations persist in some patients despite drug reduction.</p>\n<p><strong>Impulse-control disorders — the &quot;overdose hypothesis&quot;.</strong> The dose of dopaminergic drug needed to replace dopamine in the depleted dorsal striatum over-doses the relatively spared ventral striatum, which drives reward and motivation. Agonists with high D3 affinity (pramipexole, ropinirole) carry the highest risk; continuous, non-physiological stimulation and individual vulnerability (addiction traits) add to it.</p>\n<p><strong>Psychological factors.</strong> Loss of role and independence, visible disability, uncertainty about progression and stigma add to the biological vulnerability to depression and anxiety.</p>\n<h3>Clinical features in detail</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Depression:</strong> suicidal ideation is common; completed suicide is not clearly increased overall but is increased in the first years after STN-DBS. Irritability and pessimism are prominent; anhedonia overlaps with apathy.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-motor fluctuations:</strong> dysphoria, tearfulness, panic and pain can appear as each levodopa dose wears off and disappear when the next takes effect — ask the patient to keep a diary of symptoms against dose times.</li>\n<li><strong>Psychosis phenomenology:</strong> passage hallucinations are fleeting figures or animals in peripheral vision; presence hallucinations are a vivid sense that someone is nearby; pareidolia (faces in patterns) is common. Well-formed hallucinations are often silent people, children or animals, typically in the evening. <strong>Delusional jealousy (Othello syndrome)</strong> is characteristic. Auditory, tactile and olfactory hallucinations are less common. Psychosis predicts dementia, nursing-home placement and mortality.</li>\n<li><strong>Impulse-control and related behaviours:</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Behaviour</th>\n<th>Description</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Pathological gambling</td>\n<td>Including online gambling and lotteries; debt often hidden</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hypersexuality</td>\n<td>Increased demands, pornography, paraphilic behaviour, infidelity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Compulsive buying</td>\n<td>Repeated unneeded purchases</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Binge eating</td>\n<td>Weight gain, nocturnal eating, sweet craving</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Punding</td>\n<td>Repetitive, purposeless handling of objects — sorting, dismantling, collecting</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hobbyism, walkabout</td>\n<td>Excessive hobbies; aimless wandering</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dopamine dysregulation syndrome</td>\n<td>Compulsive overuse of levodopa or apomorphine beyond motor need, with hypomania, irritability and denial</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cognition:</strong> posterior cortical deficits (semantic fluency, figure copying) predict progression to dementia better than frontal-executive deficits (CamPaIGN cohort). PD dementia brings fluctuating attention, hallucinations and apathy.</li>\n<li><strong>RBD:</strong> isolated RBD converts to a synucleinopathy at about 6% per year and in over 70% by 12 years (Postuma et al., 2019) — a key prodromal marker.</li>\n<li><strong>DAWS:</strong> occurs in roughly 15–20% of patients tapering an agonist; symptoms resemble drug withdrawal in addiction and may persist for months, sometimes forcing a slower taper.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Assessment in detail</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Domain</th>\n<th>Instrument</th>\n<th>Notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Global non-motor</td>\n<td>MDS-UPDRS Part I; Non-Motor Symptoms Scale/Questionnaire</td>\n<td>Quick screen across domains</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Depression</td>\n<td>GDS-15, HADS, BDI, HAM-D</td>\n<td>Use PD-validated cut-offs; somatic items inflate scores</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Anxiety</td>\n<td>Parkinson Anxiety Scale</td>\n<td>Captures episodic and avoidance anxiety</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Apathy</td>\n<td>Starkstein Apathy Scale, Lille Apathy Rating Scale</td>\n<td>Separate from depression</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Psychosis</td>\n<td>SAPS-PD, SCOPA-PC, NPI</td>\n<td>Include minor phenomena</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ICDs</td>\n<td>QUIP / QUIP-RS</td>\n<td>Completed by patient <strong>and</strong> informant</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cognition</td>\n<td>MoCA, SCOPA-COG, ACE-III</td>\n<td>MoCA more sensitive than MMSE for PD-MCI</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sleep</td>\n<td>RBD Screening Questionnaire, Epworth Sleepiness Scale</td>\n<td>Polysomnography to confirm RBD</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Examine in a known motor state (ON or OFF) and note it; check orthostatic BP (falls, syncope with psychotropics) and vision (hallucination risk). Investigate rapidly progressive or atypical cognitive decline with neuroimaging; use EEG when fluctuating consciousness suggests seizures or delirium.</p>\n<h3>Differential diagnosis</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Presentation</th>\n<th>Consider</th>\n<th>Distinguishing point</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Low mood and slowing</td>\n<td>Apathy; hypomimia/bradyphrenia; hypothyroidism</td>\n<td>Apathy lacks sadness; motor signs without negative cognitions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fluctuating confusion + hallucinations</td>\n<td>Delirium</td>\n<td>Acute onset, inattention, identifiable precipitant</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Early dementia + hallucinations</td>\n<td>Dementia with Lewy bodies</td>\n<td>Dementia before or within one year of parkinsonism</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Parkinsonism with early falls, gaze palsy, autonomic failure</td>\n<td>PSP, MSA</td>\n<td>Poor levodopa response, red flags</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Parkinsonism in a patient on antipsychotics</td>\n<td>Drug-induced parkinsonism</td>\n<td>Symmetric onset, temporal link to D2 blocker, normal DAT-SPECT</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Episodic anxiety or dysphoria</td>\n<td>Non-motor OFF fluctuation</td>\n<td>Linked to dosing timetable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Spending, hypersexuality</td>\n<td>Mania; frontotemporal dementia</td>\n<td>Temporal link to agonist; resolves on withdrawal</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3>Management details</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Antidepressant cautions:</strong> serotonin syndrome with SSRIs plus MAO-B inhibitors is rare at usual doses but monitor; SSRIs can occasionally worsen tremor or restless legs; check sodium in the elderly (SIADH); mirtazapine helps insomnia and appetite but sedates; bupropion may help fatigue and apathy but can cause agitation.</li>\n<li><strong>ECT:</strong> risk of post-ECT delirium in patients with cognitive impairment; motor improvement is usually temporary.</li>\n<li><strong>rTMS:</strong> modest antidepressant effect in trials.</li>\n<li><strong>Clozapine practicalities:</strong> start 6.25–12.5 mg at night; most patients respond below 50 mg; blood-count monitoring as per local protocol; watch sedation, orthostasis and sialorrhoea.</li>\n<li><strong>Pimavanserin:</strong> no D2 blockade; onset over several weeks; QT prolongation; not widely available in India.</li>\n<li><strong>Quetiapine:</strong> 12.5–150 mg/day; several RCTs failed to separate from placebo; sedation and orthostasis.</li>\n<li><strong>Avoid in all Lewy body disease:</strong> typical antipsychotics, risperidone, olanzapine, aripiprazole — motor worsening and potentially fatal neuroleptic sensitivity reactions.</li>\n<li><strong>ICDs:</strong> amantadine has conflicting evidence and may itself be associated with ICDs; naltrexone is unproven; CBT has RCT support (Okai et al., 2013).</li>\n<li><strong>Rivastigmine:</strong> can transiently worsen tremor; nausea and weight loss; patch formulation better tolerated. Memantine has smaller, inconsistent effects.</li>\n<li><strong>RBD drugs:</strong> melatonin 3–12 mg at night; clonazepam 0.25–1 mg (caution: falls, sleep apnoea, cognition).</li>\n<li><strong>Insomnia:</strong> treat nocturnal akinesia, nocturia and pain; CBT-I.</li>\n<li><strong>Palliative care:</strong> advance care planning while capacity is intact; caregiver respite and support groups, especially with psychosis, ICDs or dementia.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Evidence table</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Study</th>\n<th>Design</th>\n<th>Main finding</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Reijnders et al., 2008</td>\n<td>Systematic review</td>\n<td>MDD ~17%; clinically significant depressive symptoms ~35%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SAD-PD (Richard et al., 2012)</td>\n<td>RCT, n = 115</td>\n<td>Paroxetine and venlafaxine XR improved depression; no motor worsening</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Menza et al., 2009</td>\n<td>RCT, n = 52</td>\n<td>Nortriptyline superior to placebo; paroxetine CR not</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Barone et al., 2010</td>\n<td>RCT, n ≈ 300</td>\n<td>Pramipexole improved depressive symptoms vs placebo</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dobkin et al., 2011</td>\n<td>RCT, n = 80</td>\n<td>CBT superior to clinical monitoring for depression</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Parkinson Study Group, 1999</td>\n<td>RCT, n = 60</td>\n<td>Low-dose clozapine improved psychosis; no motor worsening</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cummings et al., 2014</td>\n<td>Phase 3 RCT, n = 199</td>\n<td>Pimavanserin improved PD psychosis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>EXPRESS (Emre et al., 2004)</td>\n<td>RCT, n = 541</td>\n<td>Rivastigmine modestly improved cognition in PD dementia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>DOMINION (Weintraub et al., 2010)</td>\n<td>Cross-sectional, n = 3,090</td>\n<td>ICDs in 13.6%; agonists raised odds 2–3.5-fold</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Devos et al., 2014</td>\n<td>RCT</td>\n<td>Rivastigmine improved apathy in non-demented, non-depressed patients</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hely et al., 2008 (Sydney)</td>\n<td>20-year cohort</td>\n<td>~80% of survivors developed dementia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Postuma et al., 2019</td>\n<td>Multicentre cohort, n = 1,280</td>\n<td>Isolated RBD converted at ~6% per year</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3>Critical appraisal</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Under-recognition:</strong> structured screening substantially increases detection compared with routine consultation.</li>\n<li><strong>Measurement problem:</strong> depression scales with somatic items inflate scores in PD; PD-validated cut-offs or core mood items are needed.</li>\n<li><strong>Thin evidence base:</strong> antidepressant evidence rests on a few small, short RCTs with high placebo response.</li>\n<li><strong>Quetiapine paradox:</strong> the most widely used antipsychotic for PD psychosis has the weakest trial support, while clozapine — the best supported — is underused because of monitoring burden.</li>\n<li><strong>Pimavanserin:</strong> approval rested on one positive phase 3 trial; questions remain about effect size, QT prolongation and the mortality warning shared by all antipsychotics in elderly patients with dementia.</li>\n<li><strong>Agonist trade-off:</strong> dopamine agonists delay levodopa motor complications at a behavioural cost that often falls on families; informed consent about ICD risk before prescribing is a clinical and ethical duty.</li>\n<li><strong>DBS:</strong> excellent motor results can coexist with poor psychosocial adjustment, showing the need for psychiatric input in selection and follow-up.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Indian context</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Most patients are managed by neurologists or physicians; psychiatric syndromes are often not screened for, and families may attribute behaviour change to ageing, medication &quot;weakness&quot; or character.</li>\n<li><strong>Cost and access:</strong> clozapine needs reliable laboratory follow-up; pimavanserin is not widely available; generic SSRIs, rivastigmine and levodopa are affordable.</li>\n<li>ICD consequences (gambling, debt, sexual behaviour) carry particular stigma and marital consequences; enquire sensitively with the spouse.</li>\n<li><strong>Mental Healthcare Act 2017:</strong> capacity is decision-specific — a patient with PD dementia may retain capacity for some decisions; advance directives and nominated representatives are useful before cognition declines; admission for severe psychosis or suicidality follows MHCA provisions.</li>\n<li><strong>RPwD Act 2016:</strong> Parkinson&#39;s disease is listed among chronic neurological conditions for disability certification and benefits.</li>\n<li>DBS is available at a limited number of tertiary centres; psychiatric screening should be built into these programmes.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Recent advances</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>α-synuclein seed amplification assays</strong> (CSF, skin) detect pathological synuclein with high accuracy, including in prodromal disease (Siderowf et al., 2023), leading to proposed <strong>biological staging</strong> of PD (2024).</li>\n<li><strong>Pimavanserin</strong> as a non-dopaminergic antipsychotic (established in the US; limited availability elsewhere).</li>\n<li><strong>Telehealth and caregiver-inclusive CBT</strong> for depression and anxiety.</li>\n<li><strong>Continuous dopaminergic delivery</strong> — levodopa–carbidopa intestinal gel, subcutaneous foslevodopa–foscarbidopa, apomorphine infusion — smooths fluctuations and may reduce OFF-period anxiety and dysphoria.</li>\n<li><strong>rTMS and other neuromodulation</strong> for depression in PD (modest evidence).</li>\n<li><strong>Prodromal trials</strong> of potential disease-modifying agents in people with isolated RBD.</li>\n</ul>\n","refs":"<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Draft note:</strong> written from the author&#39;s knowledge of the literature, without uploaded source chapters. Check every figure and dose against the sources below before marking this answer <code>reviewed</code>.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<ol>\n<li>Weintraub D, Aarsland D, Biundo R, et al. Management of psychiatric and cognitive complications in Parkinson&#39;s disease. <em>BMJ</em>, 2022;379:e068718.</li>\n<li>Reijnders JS, Ehrt U, Weber WE, Aarsland D, Leentjens AF. A systematic review of prevalence studies of depression in Parkinson&#39;s disease. <em>Movement Disorders</em>, 2008;23:183–189.</li>\n<li>Broen MP, Narayen NE, Kuijf ML, et al. Prevalence of anxiety in Parkinson&#39;s disease: a systematic review and meta-analysis. <em>Movement Disorders</em>, 2016;31:1125–1133.</li>\n<li>den Brok MG, van Dalen JW, van Gool WA, et al. Apathy in Parkinson&#39;s disease: a systematic review and meta-analysis. <em>Movement Disorders</em>, 2015;30:759–769.</li>\n<li>Ravina B, Marder K, Fernandez HH, et al. Diagnostic criteria for psychosis in Parkinson&#39;s disease: report of an NINDS/NIMH work group. <em>Movement Disorders</em>, 2007;22:1061–1068.</li>\n<li>Weintraub D, Koester J, Potenza MN, et al. Impulse control disorders in Parkinson disease: a cross-sectional study of 3090 patients (DOMINION). <em>Archives of Neurology</em>, 2010;67:589–595.</li>\n<li>Richard IH, McDermott MP, Kurlan R, et al. A randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled trial of antidepressants in Parkinson disease (SAD-PD). <em>Neurology</em>, 2012;78:1229–1236.</li>\n<li>Menza M, Dobkin RD, Marin H, et al. A controlled trial of antidepressants in patients with Parkinson disease and depression. <em>Neurology</em>, 2009;72:886–892.</li>\n<li>Barone P, Poewe W, Albrecht S, et al. Pramipexole for the treatment of depressive symptoms in patients with Parkinson&#39;s disease. <em>Lancet Neurology</em>, 2010;9:573–580.</li>\n<li>Dobkin RD, Menza M, Allen LA, et al. Cognitive-behavioral therapy for depression in Parkinson&#39;s disease: a randomized, controlled trial. <em>American Journal of Psychiatry</em>, 2011;168:1066–1074.</li>\n<li>Parkinson Study Group. Low-dose clozapine for the treatment of drug-induced psychosis in Parkinson&#39;s disease. <em>New England Journal of Medicine</em>, 1999;340:757–763.</li>\n<li>Cummings J, Isaacson S, Mills R, et al. Pimavanserin for patients with Parkinson&#39;s disease psychosis. <em>Lancet</em>, 2014;383:533–540.</li>\n<li>Emre M, Aarsland D, Albanese A, et al. Rivastigmine for dementia associated with Parkinson&#39;s disease (EXPRESS). <em>New England Journal of Medicine</em>, 2004;351:2509–2518.</li>\n<li>Devos D, Moreau C, Maltête D, et al. Rivastigmine in apathetic but dementia and depression-free patients with Parkinson&#39;s disease. <em>Journal of Neurology, Neurosurgery and Psychiatry</em>, 2014;85:668–674.</li>\n<li>Hely MA, Reid WG, Adena MA, et al. The Sydney multicenter study of Parkinson&#39;s disease: the inevitability of dementia at 20 years. <em>Movement Disorders</em>, 2008;23:837–844.</li>\n<li>Postuma RB, Iranzo A, Hu M, et al. Risk and predictors of dementia and parkinsonism in idiopathic REM sleep behaviour disorder. <em>Brain</em>, 2019;142:744–759.</li>\n<li>Braak H, Del Tredici K, Rüb U, et al. Staging of brain pathology related to sporadic Parkinson&#39;s disease. <em>Neurobiology of Aging</em>, 2003;24:197–211.</li>\n<li>Collerton D, Perry E, McKeith I. Why people see things that are not there: a novel Perception and Attention Deficit model for recurrent complex visual hallucinations. <em>Behavioral and Brain Sciences</em>, 2005;28:737–757.</li>\n<li>Siderowf A, Concha-Marambio L, Lafontant DE, et al. Assessment of heterogeneity among participants in the Parkinson&#39;s Progression Markers Initiative cohort using α-synuclein seed amplification. <em>Lancet Neurology</em>, 2023;22:407–417.</li>\n<li>Bharucha NE, Bharucha EP, Bharucha AE, et al. Prevalence of Parkinson&#39;s disease in the Parsi community of Bombay, India. <em>Archives of Neurology</em>, 1988;45:1321–1323.</li>\n<li>David AS, Fleminger S, Kopelman MD, et al. (eds). <em>Lishman&#39;s Organic Psychiatry</em>, 4th edition — chapter on movement disorders.</li>\n<li>Government of India. Mental Healthcare Act, 2017; Rights of Persons with Disabilities Act, 2016.</li>\n</ol>\n"}},"byQuestion":{"p3-303f02bd":"parkinsons-disease-psychiatric-aspects","p3-fdf72d0c":"parkinsons-disease-psychiatric-aspects","p3-f4a09b39":"parkinsons-disease-psychiatric-aspects","p3-a0ea515e":"parkinsons-disease-psychiatric-aspects","p3-dc17344a":"parkinsons-disease-psychiatric-aspects","p3-868b6f08":"parkinsons-disease-psychiatric-aspects"}};
